# Minimal Code Modification Principle

## Context
- When creating new code to implement functional requirements
- When modifying existing code to fix bugs or meet user's new requirements
- When working with Obsidian and CodeMirror related development

## Requirements
- Use the minimum amount of code to implement all required functionality when creating new code
- Make only necessary minimal changes when modifying code
- New features must be elegantly designed and align with the overall architecture
- Strictly maintain the integrity of existing functionality and layout styles
- Follow the development standards and API usage of Obsidian and CodeMirror

## Examples
<example>
// Excellent code modification example:
// Original code:
function displayFileName(file) {
  return file.name;
}

// Minimal modification (only added necessary code):
function displayFileName(file) {
  return file.name.endsWith('.md') ? file.name.slice(0, -3) : file.name;
}
</example>

<example type="invalid">
// Poor code modification example:
// Original code:
function displayFileName(file) {
  return file.name;
}

// Excessive modification (rewrote the entire functionality, changed original behavior):
function displayFileName(file) {
  const formattedName = formatFileName(file.name);
  renderFileIcon(file.type);
  updateFileHistory(file);
  return `<span class="file-name">${formattedName}</span>`;
}
</example>

## Critical Rules
<critical>
  - Never change the behavior or output of existing functionality
  - Do not modify original layout and UI styles
  - Code additions must follow the principle of minimal invasiveness
  - Strictly adhere to Obsidian and CodeMirror API specifications
  - New feature designs must be elegant and seamlessly integrate with existing systems
</critical>

